{% extends "_base.html.j2" %}

{% block title %}AI Act Control Objectives{% endblock %}

{% block content %}
  <div class="co-header">
    <h1>AI Act Control Objectives</h1>
    <p>What an AI system has to satisfy under the EU AI Act, and which of it to do first.
       The catalogue is the same for everyone; what a particular system owes depends on
       what it is and what its assessor found could go wrong with it.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="co-doors">
    <a class="co-door" href="{{ root_path }}/objectives">
      <span class="co-door-count">{{ objectives_count }}</span>
      <h2>Control objectives</h2>
      <p>The catalogue: {{ objectives_count }} objectives under {{ macros_count }} macro
         requirements, each with its legal basis and its standards grounding. Read it,
         search it, no system involved.</p>
      <span class="co-door-go">Explore the catalogue →</span>
    </a>

    <a class="co-door co-door--primary" href="{{ root_path }}/projects">
      <span class="co-door-count">{{ projects_count }}</span>
      <h2>Projects</h2>
      <p>{% if projects_count %}{{ projects_count }} system{{ 's' if projects_count != 1 }} under assessment.{% else %}No systems under assessment yet.{% endif %}
         Upload a system's AI Card, rank the risks it carries, and it works out what
         answers them and where to start.</p>
      <span class="co-door-go">
        {%- if projects_count %}Open the projects →{% else %}Assess a system →{% endif %}
      </span>
    </a>
  </div>

  <div class="qf-section">
    <h2>How an assessment runs</h2>
    <ol class="co-steps">
      <li><strong>Upload the system's AI Card</strong> from the qualification app. The
          card is the system's filled AIRO graph: what it is, and the risks its assessor
          identified.</li>
      <li><strong>Rank the risks</strong> 1 to 5 for this system. Which risks matter
          here is the assessor's call, and it is the one judgement a model does not
          make for you.</li>
      <li><strong>The risks are mapped to the objectives</strong> that would mitigate
          them, each on a quote from the risk's own chain.</li>
      <li><strong>Read the tiers.</strong> The objectives answering the worst risks
          become Tier 1: no more than seven, so it is a place to start rather than
          another list.</li>
    </ol>
  </div>
{% endblock %}
